<DocsLayout title="Merging accounts">
  <h1>Merging accounts</h1>
  <p>
    A person can end up with two accounts in one project. One example is a
    Gmail sign-up (with the address <code>bob-at-gmail.com@accounts.example.com</code>)
    alongside a username account <code>bob</code> (<code>bob@accounts.example.com</code>).
    A merge joins them under one account <strong>without deleting
    anything</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>What a merge does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Whoever asks chooses the <strong>survivor</strong>; the server never
      picks. The other account is <strong>retired</strong>: it becomes
      <code>USER_STATUS_DEACTIVATED</code>, and <code>merged_into_user_id</code>
      names the survivor. All its sessions are revoked, it never signs in again,
      and it can never be reactivated: <code>ReactivateUser</code> refuses it,
      and SCIM no longer sees it. Its row, and everything stored under its id,
      stays.
    </li>
    <li>
      Everything that lets the person sign in the way they did moves to the
      survivor, in one transaction:
      <ul>
        <li>the retired account's <strong>linked providers</strong> (Google, Apple, …), always;</li>
        <li>its <strong>username</strong>, <strong>password</strong> and <strong>email</strong>, each only where the survivor has none of its own, so <code>bob</code> now signs in to the survivor;</li>
        <li>with <code>take_address</code>, its account address. The survivor's old address moves to the retired account, so neither is freed for someone else.</li>
      </ul>
      <strong>Passkeys do not move.</strong> A passkey is bound to the account
      it was registered for, so it would not sign in to the survivor. Register
      passkeys again on the survivor.
      If either account changes while the merge runs (another merge, a
      deactivation), nothing is written and the call answers
      <code>ABORTED</code>; try again.
    </li>
    <li>
      An email goes to every address either account had, saying which account
      was merged into which, so someone who did not ask for it hears at once.
    </li>
    <li>
      A <code>user.merged</code> event names the retired account and carries
      <code>merged_into_user_id</code>. Applications move whatever they hold
      under the retired id to the survivor.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Turning it on</h2>
  <p>
    Self-service merging is <strong>off</strong> until you set
    <code>GATEWAY_ACCOUNT_MERGE_ENABLED=true</code>. A merge retires an
    account for good, and applications learn about it only through the
    <code>user.merged</code> webhook. Subscribe every application that stores
    data under user ids to <code>user.merged</code> first. The admin
    <code>MergeUsers</code> works either way.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can merge</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The person</strong>, with <code>MergeAccounts</code> called from the
      survivor's session, with the client assurance a password sign-in needs.
      The survivor is checked first, so an account that could not take another
      in learns nothing about the other's password. They prove they control the other account with its
      identifier (email or username) and password, checked by the same code as
      a password sign-in: the access mode, the lockout, the account's status,
      the verified-email requirement and the login policy all apply, with the
      same refusals and audit entries, on the per-IP login limit. An account
      protected by a second factor (enrolled two-step, or a policy requiring
      one) cannot be merged by password alone: turn two-step off there first.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>An admin</strong>, with <code>MergeUsers</code>, naming both
      accounts. No password is asked.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What cannot be merged</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An account into itself.</li>
    <li>An account that is not active.</li>
    <li>An anonymous account: upgrade it instead.</li>
    <li>A managed child's account, which stays with its guardian.</li>
    <li>An account that is a guardian: move its children first.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>These refusals answer <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>.</p>
</DocsLayout>
